/*
RMSTEST_35 - the elevation diagonal step at a SECOND SIZE, with NO player lands
preview-design.md Sec.15 item 11(b)

WHY THIS EXISTS - RMSTEST_32 IS CONFOUNDED
RMSTEST_32 measured the favoured:disfavoured ratio across the y = x diagonal at
Normal (200) with 8 players, and got 2.25 / 2.40 / 2.08 - call it 2.24:1 -
against the 18:1 that RMSTEST_22a measured at Tiny (120). That is an order of
magnitude, it is stable across three distinct maps, and it cannot be dismissed.

But RMSTEST_32 changed TWO things at once against RMSTEST_22a:
  - map size, 120 -> 200
  - player lands, absent -> eight present
and Sec.6.2's elevation rule excludes tiles within 9 of a player origin. Eight
exclusion discs sit on a ring, and that ring CROSSES the diagonal, so the
exclusion and the step are not independent. RMSTEST_32's own prediction 3 said
exactly this would happen. Its band profiles came back noisy and inconsistent
between runs, which is what eight holes on a ring looks like.

So the 2.24:1 cannot be attributed. This script changes ONE thing.

METHOD
Byte-for-byte RMSTEST_22a - same base terrain, same create_elevation, same
declared numbers, no player lands, no create_player_lands at all - generated at
Normal (200) instead of Tiny (120). The declared tile budget is deliberately
NOT scaled: the reading is a RATIO between two halves of the map, which is
density-independent, and holding the declared numbers fixed keeps the only
difference the map size.

READ IT WITH
  python probe_scenario.py "<scenario>" --rows ELEVATION --bands 20
  and the diagonal split - count elevated tiles with y > x against y < x.
  The ratio is the reading. --rows gives the marginals as corroboration:
  mean y should sit above 0.5 and mean x below it by a similar margin.

PREDICTIONS, RECORDED BEFORE THE RUN
1. Ratio near 18:1. Then the step does NOT weaken with map size, and the entire
   drop to 2.24:1 in RMSTEST_32 is caused by the player-land exclusion. Sec.6.2
   would then be wrong to state the step and the exclusion as independent rules
   composed together, and item 11(b) becomes a real modelling change.
2. Ratio near 2.24:1. Then the strength is SIZE-dependent and nothing currently
   models that. The 18:1 constant would be a Tiny-specific fit, and every map
   size would need its own - which is a far worse outcome for Sec.6.2 than 1.
3. Ratio somewhere between. Both effects are real and partially additive; the
   exclusion cannot then be separated without a third run, and the honest thing
   is to say so rather than split the difference.

4. Item 11(a)'s residual - the disfavoured half's internal gradient running the
   WRONG way (0.09 next to the diagonal, 0.21 in the far corner at Tiny) -
   should reappear cleanly here, because this run has no player lands to muddy
   the bands. RMSTEST_32's band profiles were too noisy to judge it. If it does
   not reappear in a clean run at a second size, item 11(a) closes as a
   Tiny-specific artefact.

GENERATE AT NORMAL (200) WITH ANY PLAYER COUNT - there are no player lands, so
the count does not enter. RUN IT THREE TIMES, and REGENERATE BETWEEN SAVES:
RMSTEST_32's first attempt exported the same generated map three times, which
looked like three runs and was one. The check is that the elevated-tile totals
differ between runs; identical totals mean identical maps.

SECOND CONFIGURATION, ADDED 2026-08-04 - ALSO GENERATE THIS AT LUDICROUS (480),
SIX TIMES. Item 11(a) established that the elevation bias weakens within ~12
tiles of the map edge and that the width does NOT scale with map size - but that
rests on exactly two sizes, 120 and 200. Two points define a line, and this
project has just been caught by two points at one end of a range: Sec.15 item 16,
where cf -5 and cf 0 made a merely mis-parameterised model look refuted. A third
size at the far end of the range is the cheap guard against repeating it.

SIX runs rather than three at this size, deliberately. The tile budget here is
absolute, so at 480 coverage falls to about 0.9% against 6.2% at Normal, and the
disfavoured near-edge cells are already the thinnest part of the reading. The
ratio is density-normalised and therefore unbiased by the sparseness; only the
noise grows, and runs are what fix noise.

Read it exactly as the Normal runs are read, then compare WHERE the ratio
recovers - not what it recovers to. If recovery still completes by ~12 tiles the
band is absolute and the constant can be pinned. If it stretches toward ~29
tiles (12 x 480/200) it is proportional after all and Sec.6.2 needs a dim term.
*/

<PLAYER_SETUP>
  random_placement

<LAND_GENERATION>
  base_terrain GRASS

<ELEVATION_GENERATION>

  create_elevation 1
  {
    number_of_clumps 500
    number_of_tiles 2000
    spacing 1
  }

/* NO <CLIFF_GENERATION> HEADER - an empty one generates cliffs. */

<TERRAIN_GENERATION>

<OBJECTS_GENERATION>

<CONNECTION_GENERATION>
